// Grade 6 passages (validated by live-patches/grade-journey/validate.mjs). Built by validate.mjs --write.
export const PASSAGES = [{"id":"g6-u1-p01","unit":"G6-U1","pool":"practice","title":"The Crooked Wave","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Tavita Faleolo had spent the whole spring waiting for the mural. The town had chosen the long brick wall of the laundromat on Fourth Street, and an artist named Lena Ortiz was leading the project with twelve volunteers from the middle school. Tavita had signed up first, before anyone else even knew about the sign-up sheet.\n\nThe design showed the harbor at sunrise: fishing boats, gulls, the old stone pier and a huge blue wave rolling across the bottom of the wall. Lena divided the wall into a grid of chalk squares and gave each volunteer a section. Tavita was thrilled to receive the very center of the wave.\n\nTavita was the kind of person who erased a math problem three times if her numbers looked messy. She outlined her section carefully, mixed her blues until they matched the sketch exactly and painted in slow, even strokes. By the end of the second Saturday, her part of the wave looked almost like a photograph.\n\nOn the third Saturday, a delivery truck rumbled past just as she was painting the curling top edge of the wave. The ladder wobbled, her arm jerked, and a thick stripe of dark blue slashed sideways across the foam she had spent two hours perfecting. Tavita stared at the stripe, horrified. It looked like a crack running through the whole picture.\n\nShe climbed down without a word and sat on the curb with her face in her hands. When Lena came over, Tavita told her she wanted to paint the entire section white and start again, even if it took every remaining weekend.\n\nLena studied the wall for a long moment instead of answering. Then she asked Tavita to walk across the street with her. From the far sidewalk, the stripe looked different. It ran in the same direction as the wind in the sketch, and it made the top of the wave seem to lean forward, as if it were about to crash onto the pier.\n\n\"Real waves aren't perfect,\" Lena said. \"That line gives your wave motion. Nobody else's section has that.\" She explained that every mural she had ever painted contained mistakes, and that some of her favorite parts had started as accidents.\n\nTavita wasn't convinced at first. But she climbed back up the ladder, and instead of covering the stripe, she added two thinner lines beside it and a spray of white dots above it. Gradually, the accident turned into a curling crest of water that seemed to be moving.\n\nWhen the mural was finished in June, the town held a small celebration on Fourth Street. People pointed at the boats and the gulls, but many of them stopped in front of the wave. An old fisherman told Tavita it was the only painted wave he had ever seen that looked wet. A little boy asked how she had made it look like it was moving.\n\nTavita thought about the truck, the wobbling ladder and the afternoon she had spent on the curb. She almost explained that it had been a mistake. Instead, she smiled and said that sometimes the best part of a picture is the part you didn't plan.\n\nThat summer, she still erased her math problems when they looked messy. But whenever she walked past the laundromat, she looked at the crooked line at the top of the wave and felt proud of it.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"Which statement best expresses the story's theme?","options":["Careful planning is the only way to make good art.","A mistake can turn into something valuable if you work with it.","Group projects are easier when one person does the work."],"answer":0},{"type":"main","q":"Which is the best summary of the story?","options":["A girl quits a mural project after a truck knocks her ladder over.","A girl ruins a mural, then paints a new wall by herself in secret.","A girl smears her mural section, then turns the mistake into its best part."],"answer":2},{"type":"literal","q":"What did Tavita want to do right after the accident?","options":["leave the project and go home for the day","paint the entire section white and start again","ask Lena to give her a smaller section"],"answer":1,"evidence":"When Lena came over, Tavita told her she wanted to paint the entire section white and start again, even if it took every remaining weekend."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does Lena take Tavita across the street?","options":["to let her see the stripe from a distance","to show her another volunteer's section","to get her away from the noisy traffic"],"answer":0}],"grade":6,"words":566,"auto":{"fk":6.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u1-p02","unit":"G6-U1","pool":"practice","title":"The Brick","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Every May, Harbor View Middle School held a cardboard boat regatta in the town pool. The rules were simple: each team of three could use only cardboard, duct tape and paint, and at least one person had to paddle the boat across the pool and back. Teams that finished won ribbons. Teams that sank won applause and a long, cold swim to the ladder.\n\nNnamdi Eze had been waiting for the regatta since fourth grade. When his science teacher put him on a team with Paloma Ruiz and a quiet new student named Jalen, Nnamdi already had a plan. He spread his sketch across a lab table during their first meeting. It showed a long, narrow boat with a pointed nose, like a racing canoe.\n\nNarrow boats were faster, he explained confidently, because they had less water to push out of the way.\n\nPaloma tilted her head and pointed out that cardboard canoes tended to tip over, since her cousin's team had tried one the previous year. She drew a quick rectangle in the corner of his page. \"What about something wide and flat, like a raft with walls?\"\n\nNnamdi barely glanced at her drawing before announcing that it was a box, not a boat. Jalen said nothing at all, and since nobody argued, the canoe design won.\n\nFor three weeks, the team folded, taped and painted after school. Nnamdi made most of the decisions. When Jalen suggested taping the seams on both the inside and the outside, Nnamdi said it would waste tape. When Paloma asked whether the nose was too thin, he told her she worried too much. In the end the boat looked sharp, painted red with a gold stripe, and they named it the Comet.\n\nThe week before the regatta, the gym teacher let teams test their boats in the pool. Nnamdi climbed into the Comet first. He pushed off from the wall, took two strong strokes and felt the boat lean to the left. He leaned right to correct it, and the boat rolled even farther to the right. Water poured over the edge. Within ten seconds, the Comet was a red puddle of cardboard, and Nnamdi was treading water while the other teams tried hard not to laugh.\n\nThat evening, Nnamdi sat on his back steps with the soggy remains of his sketch. He thought about the rectangle Paloma had drawn and the extra tape Jalen had wanted. His teammates had seen the problems long before he had. He had simply refused to listen.\n\nThe next day, he brought a blank sheet of paper to their meeting instead of a new drawing. \"I was wrong about the canoe,\" he admitted. \"We have five days left, so what do you think we should build?\"\n\nPaloma's raft became the base. Jalen, it turned out, had built model boats with his grandfather for years, and he showed them how to fold cardboard into thick ribs that kept the bottom stiff. Nnamdi made a paddle from a broom handle and two layers of cardboard. They taped every seam twice, inside and out.\n\nOn the day of the regatta, their new boat looked a little like a floating shoebox. A boy on another team called it the Brick, and the name stuck. Paloma paddled it across the pool and back, slowly and steadily, while two sleek canoes rolled over beside her. The Brick finished fourth out of eleven boats, and it was still dry inside when she climbed out.\n\nThey did not win first place, but when the judges gave out a special ribbon for the best design change, they called the Brick's team forward. Nnamdi let Jalen carry the ribbon. It seemed fair, since a good idea had been sitting quietly beside him all along.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"Which statement best expresses the story's theme?","options":["The fastest design is always the best one to choose.","Listening to others can turn a failure into a success.","Winning first place matters more than working as a team."],"answer":1},{"type":"main","q":"Which is the best summary of the story?","options":["A boy's boat sinks, he listens to his team, and their new boat finishes.","A boy builds a fast canoe that wins first place at the school regatta.","A boy quits his team after the other students laugh at his sinking boat."],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What happened during the pool test of the Comet?","options":["the paddle snapped in two","the paint washed off","water poured over the edge"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Water poured over the edge."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does Nnamdi bring a blank sheet of paper to the meeting?","options":["to show he has run out of drawings","to show he is ready to hear new ideas","to show he plans to leave the team"],"answer":1}],"grade":6,"words":627,"auto":{"fk":5.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u1-c01","unit":"G6-U1","pool":"check","title":"The Map on the Wall","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"When Grandpa Ray moved into the small apartment above Lena's family's garage, he brought only a few boxes and one enormous rolled-up map. He unrolled it on the kitchen table on a rainy Saturday, holding the corners down with coffee mugs. It was a hand-drawn map of the neighborhood, made in ink that had faded to the color of weak tea.\n\n\"I drew this when I was your age,\" he told Lena. \"My teacher said we should map the places that mattered to us.\"\n\nLena leaned closer. She recognized the river and the shape of the main road, but almost nothing else matched the neighborhood she knew. Where the grocery store stood now, the map showed a field labeled \"Baseball, Sundays.\" The parking garage downtown was a bakery called Kowalski's. A tiny star marked a spot by the river with the words \"Best place to think.\"\n\n\"Everything's gone,\" Lena said. She meant it kindly, but Grandpa Ray's face fell a little, and she wished she could take the words back.\n\nThe next weekend, she asked him to walk the neighborhood with her and the map. They moved slowly because of his bad hip, stopping often. At each stop, he told her what used to be there, and she told him what was there now. The bakery had become a parking garage, but the corner shop next to it still sold the same poppy-seed rolls, because the baker's granddaughter ran it. The baseball field had become a grocery store, but the kids who once played there had pooled their money years later to build the small park behind it.\n\nBy the river, they found the star. The spot was still there: a flat rock under a willow tree, with the water sliding past. Grandpa Ray sat down on it carefully and closed his eyes. Lena sat beside him. Neither of them said anything for a long time, and Lena realized it really was a good place to think.\n\nThat evening, Lena took out a large sheet of paper and began a map of her own. She drew the grocery store and the parking garage, the corner shop and the little park. She drew her school, the library where she volunteered and the bus stop where she met her best friend every morning. Then, by the river, she drew a small star and copied her grandfather's words beside it.\n\nWhen she showed him, Grandpa Ray studied it for a long time. \"Someday,\" he said, \"someone will look at this and say everything's gone.\"\n\n\"Maybe,\" said Lena. \"But I'll show them where to look.\"\n\nThey hung the two maps side by side on the wall of his apartment. Visitors always noticed how different they were. Lena always pointed out how the river ran through both.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"Which theme does the story develop?","options":["Old maps are more accurate than modern ones.","Cities should protect every old building forever.","Places change, but their memories and meaning can last."],"answer":2},{"type":"main","q":"Which sentence best summarizes the story?","options":["Lena and her grandfather compare his old map to today's town, and she makes her own.","Lena helps her grandfather move into a new apartment and unpack his heavy boxes.","Lena and her grandfather search the river for a treasure that is marked by a star."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that some things in the neighborhood stayed the same?","options":["Where the grocery store stood now, the map showed a field labeled \"Baseball, Sundays.\"","The spot was still there: a flat rock under a willow tree, with the water sliding past.","He unrolled it on the kitchen table on a rainy Saturday, holding the corners down with coffee mugs."],"answer":1,"evidence":"The spot was still there: a flat rock under a willow tree, with the water sliding past."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does Lena wish she could take back her words about the neighborhood?","options":["She realizes the map is missing several streets.","She worries that she has upset her parents.","She sees that the words hurt her grandfather."],"answer":2},{"type":"craft","q":"What does the river most likely represent in the final paragraph?","options":["the link between the past and the present","the danger of floods in the neighborhood","the distance between Lena and her friend"],"answer":0}],"grade":6,"words":465,"auto":{"fk":5.4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u1-c02","unit":"G6-U1","pool":"check","title":"The Bridge That Held","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Kwame was sure his bridge design was the best in the class. The assignment for science was simple: in teams of three, build a bridge from craft sticks and glue that could span a gap of forty centimeters. On Friday, each bridge would be tested by hanging a bucket from its center and adding sand until it collapsed. Kwame had drawn his design the night before, a long, elegant arch that looked like the bridges in his favorite travel videos.\n\nHis teammates were Rosa, who said little in class but filled her notebooks with sketches, and Ben, who mostly wanted to finish quickly. When Kwame showed them his drawing, Rosa tilted her head. \"The arch looks great,\" she said, \"but I think it needs triangles on the sides. Triangles don't bend the way rectangles do.\"\n\n\"Triangles make it look like a fence,\" Kwame said. \"Ours should look like a real bridge.\" Ben shrugged and agreed with Kwame, mostly because it meant less cutting. Rosa didn't argue; she simply wrote something in her notebook and started gluing.\n\nFor three days, they built Kwame's arch, and it was beautiful. Other teams walked over to admire it, and Kwame enjoyed every compliment. On Thursday, during a practice test, they hung the bucket and poured in sand. At just over two kilograms, the arch twisted sideways and snapped with a sound like a cracking knuckle. Sticks scattered across the floor.\n\nKwame stared at the scattered pieces and felt his face grow hot. Rosa knelt and picked up two of the broken sticks. \"It didn't break in the middle,\" she said quietly. \"It twisted. Nothing was holding the sides steady.\"\n\nThat evening, Kwame looked up bridge engineering online. He learned that many real bridges use a frame called a truss, which is built from connected triangles. A triangle keeps its shape under pressure because its sides support one another. The fence-like design he had rejected was used in railroad bridges that carried trains weighing thousands of tons.\n\nThe next morning, he arrived early and found Rosa already at their table. He handed her his notebook, where he had redrawn the arch with a row of triangles along each side. \"I should have listened to you on Monday,\" he said. Rosa smiled and opened her own notebook to the page she had sketched that first day. Her drawing was almost the same as his.\n\nThey rebuilt the bridge in one long afternoon, working fast and talking constantly. Even Ben got interested and suggested doubling the sticks at the base. On Friday, their bridge held nine kilograms of sand before it finally gave way, the second-highest total in the class. Kwame barely noticed the score. He was too busy asking Rosa what she thought they should build next.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"Which theme does the story develop?","options":["Beautiful designs are always stronger than plain ones.","Good ideas can come from anyone, so it pays to listen.","Working alone is faster than working in a team."],"answer":1},{"type":"main","q":"Which sentence best summarizes the story?","options":["Kwame's arch wins the class contest because it looks like a famous bridge.","Rosa leaves Kwame's team after he refuses to use her triangle design.","Kwame's arch fails, so he takes Rosa's advice and the team builds a stronger bridge."],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Rosa's suggestion was based on sound science?","options":["A triangle keeps its shape under pressure because its sides support one another.","Other teams walked over to admire it, and Kwame enjoyed every compliment.","Ben shrugged and agreed with Kwame, mostly because it meant less cutting."],"answer":0,"evidence":"A triangle keeps its shape under pressure because its sides support one another."},{"type":"literal","q":"How much sand did the rebuilt bridge hold on Friday?","options":["two kilograms","nine kilograms","twelve kilograms"],"answer":1,"evidence":"On Friday, their bridge held nine kilograms of sand before it finally gave way, the second-highest total in the class."},{"type":"inference","q":"What does Rosa's notebook drawing reveal at the end?","options":["She had copied Kwame's first drawing.","She planned to build her own bridge.","She had the right idea from the start."],"answer":2}],"grade":6,"words":463,"auto":{"fk":5.1,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u1-c03","unit":"G6-U1","pool":"check","title":"One Stone a Day","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In a valley where the wind blew hard every winter, there lived a girl named Ines and her grandmother. Their small farm sat at the bottom of a slope, and each year, when the snow melted, water rushed down the hill and carried away part of their garden. The soil that remained grew thinner and stonier with every spring.\n\nOne morning, Ines found her grandmother carrying a flat stone across the field. The old woman set it down at the edge of the slope, pressed it into the mud with her boot and walked back toward the house.\n\nWhen Ines asked what she was doing, her grandmother explained that she was building a wall.\n\nInes looked doubtfully at the single stone, then at the long, steep slope above it, and pointed out that one stone would never stop the water. \"No,\" her grandmother agreed, \"but tomorrow there will be two.\"\n\nEvery day after that, her grandmother placed one stone. Some days she found one lying in the field, and on other days she pulled one from the icy creek. When it rained, she placed her stone anyway, wrapped in a shawl that dripped all over the kitchen floor afterward. Ines considered the whole project a waste of time, and the neighbors privately agreed. A man passing on the road once laughed and called out that she would need a hundred years.\n\nBy the end of the first summer, the stones formed a low line no taller than a cat. By the end of the second, the line had become a wall that reached Ines's knees and stretched halfway across the field. That spring, when the snow melted, the water struck the wall and turned aside. For the first time Ines could remember, the garden stayed where it was planted.\n\nDuring the third summer, her grandmother began to move more slowly. Her hands ached in the mornings, and some days she could not lift even a small stone. Ines watched her sit by the window and look out at the unfinished wall. The next morning, Ines went to the creek, chose a flat gray stone and carried it to the end of the line. She pressed it into the earth with her boot, the way she had seen it done hundreds of times.\n\nShe did not say anything about it, and neither did her grandmother. But the next day, Ines placed another.\n\nYears later, the wall ran the full length of the slope, and the farm grew more vegetables than the two of them could eat. Travelers on the road sometimes stopped to admire it and asked who had built such a long wall. Ines always gave the same answer. \"My grandmother placed the first stone,\" she said. \"Then we built it one day at a time.\"\n\nWhen a neighbor's child asked how anyone could finish such a huge job, Ines handed her a small stone and pointed toward the slope.","questions":[{"type":"theme","q":"Which theme does the story develop?","options":["Small efforts repeated over time can reach big goals.","Neighbors usually know the best way to farm.","Strong winds are the greatest danger to a farm."],"answer":0},{"type":"main","q":"Which sentence best summarizes the story?","options":["A grandmother hires neighbors to build a wall in a single summer.","A grandmother starts a wall one stone a day, and Ines carries it on.","Ines builds a wall alone after her grandmother moves to the city."],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that the wall began to work?","options":["Some days she found one lying in the field, and on other days she pulled one from the icy creek.","Ines considered the whole project a waste of time, and the neighbors privately agreed.","That spring, when the snow melted, the water struck the wall and turned aside."],"answer":2,"evidence":"That spring, when the snow melted, the water struck the wall and turned aside."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does Ines begin placing stones during the third summer?","options":["She wants to carry on her grandmother's work.","She hopes to win a bet with the neighbors.","She was told to finish the wall by winter."],"answer":0},{"type":"craft","q":"Why does the author repeat the image of pressing a stone in with a boot?","options":["to show that the ground in the valley is very soft","to show that Ines is following her grandmother's example","to show that Ines dislikes working in the wet mud"],"answer":1}],"grade":6,"words":490,"auto":{"fk":5.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u2-p01","unit":"G6-U2","pool":"practice","title":"Talking in Rumbles","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Stand near a herd of elephants, and you might hear trumpets, snorts and squeals. What you probably will not hear is most of the conversation. Many elephant calls are rumbles so low that human ears can barely detect them, and some fall below the range of human hearing altogether. For years, scientists had no idea how much elephants were saying to each other.\n\nThe first clue came in 1984. A biologist named Katy Payne was watching Asian elephants at a zoo in Portland, Oregon. Standing near their enclosure, she felt a strange throbbing in the air, like the deepest notes of a pipe organ, even though she heard nothing unusual. She suspected the elephants were making sounds too low for her to hear. Payne returned with equipment that could record very low sounds. When the recordings were sped up, the hidden calls became audible. The elephants were producing infrasound, which is sound below about 20 vibrations per second, the lowest pitch most people can hear.\n\nLow sounds have a special advantage. High-pitched sounds fade quickly as they travel through air and bounce off trees and hills. Low-pitched sounds travel much farther. Researchers have found that elephant rumbles can carry for several kilometers, especially in the cool, still air of evening. That means elephants separated by a long distance may still be able to keep track of one another.\n\nScientists who study wild elephants in Africa have learned that rumbles carry many kinds of messages. Some rumbles seem to say \"let's go,\" and the family moves off together. Others greet relatives after a separation. Some warn the group of danger, such as lions or people. Mothers and calves rumble back and forth to stay in contact in tall grass.\n\nElephants may also listen with their feet. In the 1990s, a researcher named Caitlin O'Connell-Rodwell was studying elephants in Namibia when she noticed something odd. Sometimes a whole group would suddenly freeze. Some elephants leaned forward, and some lifted one foot off the ground, as if they were concentrating. O'Connell-Rodwell suspected that the animals were sensing vibrations in the ground. A powerful rumble sends waves through the soil as well as through the air.\n\nTo test her idea, she and her team played recorded elephant calls through the ground instead of through the air. The elephants responded to the vibrations, and they reacted differently to calls from familiar elephants than to calls from strangers. Elephant feet contain many nerve endings that are sensitive to vibration, and the bones of the legs may carry signals up toward the ears.\n\nThese discoveries matter for more than curiosity. Wildlife managers use microphones that record rumbles to count elephants in thick forests, where the animals are hard to see. Understanding how elephants communicate helps people protect them. The forest is not as quiet as it seems. It is full of conversations that we are only beginning to hear.","questions":[{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that people miss most of what elephants say?","options":["Many elephant calls are rumbles so low that human ears can barely detect them, and some fall below the range of human hearing altogether.","Researchers have found that elephant rumbles can carry for several kilometers, especially in the cool, still air of evening.","Wildlife managers use microphones that record rumbles to count elephants in thick forests, where the animals are hard to see."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Many elephant calls are rumbles so low that human ears can barely detect them, and some fall below the range of human hearing altogether."},{"type":"vocab","q":"Based on the passage, what is \"infrasound\"?","options":["sound that travels only through water","sound made by a large pipe organ","sound too low for most people to hear"],"answer":2,"evidence":"The elephants were producing infrasound, which is sound below about 20 vibrations per second, the lowest pitch most people can hear."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why did some elephants in Namibia lift one foot off the ground?","options":["to rest after a long walk","to sense vibrations in the soil","to warn calves about deep mud"],"answer":1},{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Elephants share messages with low sounds that travel through air and ground.","Elephants in zoos are much louder than elephants that live in the wild.","Elephants mostly use their trunks to trumpet loud warnings at lions."],"answer":0}],"grade":6,"words":486,"auto":{"fk":7.5,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u2-p02","unit":"G6-U2","pool":"practice","title":"How Honeybees Choose a Home","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In late spring, a crowded honeybee colony often does something dramatic. The old queen and about half the workers leave the hive in a swirling cloud called a swarm. Within minutes, thousands of bees settle in a tight cluster on a tree branch, where they may hang for hours or even days. They cannot stay there for long, because a branch offers no shelter from rain or winter cold. The swarm must find a new home, and it does so in a surprisingly democratic way.\n\nThe search is carried out by a few hundred scout bees, usually the oldest and most experienced foragers. They fly out in every direction, looking for a hollow tree or a similar cavity. A good home must meet several requirements. It should hold roughly forty liters, have a small entrance that is easy to defend and sit several meters above the ground.\n\nWhen a scout finds a promising site, she returns to the cluster and performs a waggle dance on the surface of the swarm. The direction of her dance tells other bees which way to fly, and the length of each waggle tells them how far. Most importantly, the energy of her dance reflects the quality of the site. A scout that has found an excellent cavity dances many times with great enthusiasm. A scout that has found a mediocre one dances only a few times and then stops.\n\nOther scouts watch these dances, fly out to inspect the advertised sites and then return to dance for the ones they like. Over time, the best site attracts more and more supporters, while weaker sites lose them. Scientists who have studied swarms call this process a debate, because many options are considered and compared before the group reaches a decision.\n\nThe biologist Thomas Seeley spent years studying swarms on an island off the coast of Maine, where there were no natural tree cavities. He set up nest boxes of different sizes and watched which ones the scouts chose. Again and again, the swarms picked the box that best matched what bees need. Seeley found that the decision is reached when about fifteen or more scouts gather at the same site at the same time. When the scouts sense this crowd, which scientists call a quorum, they return to the swarm and signal the bees to warm up their flight muscles. Soon the entire swarm lifts off and flies to its new home.\n\nWhat makes this system work is that no single bee is in charge. Even the queen does not make the choice. Each scout shares information, checks the work of others and gradually stops dancing for a site, so that no bee stays stuck on her first opinion. Seeley has argued that human groups, such as committees, could learn from the way bees gather options, encourage open debate and avoid deciding too quickly.","questions":[{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that bees compare the quality of different sites?","options":["They fly out in every direction, looking for a hollow tree or a similar cavity.","Most importantly, the energy of her dance reflects the quality of the site.","Within minutes, thousands of bees settle in a tight cluster on a tree branch, where they may hang for hours or even days."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Most importantly, the energy of her dance reflects the quality of the site."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In paragraph 5, what does the word \"quorum\" mean?","options":["a special dance that shows distance","a kind of tree used for nesting","a large enough group at one site"],"answer":2,"evidence":"When the scouts sense this crowd, which scientists call a quorum, they return to the swarm and signal the bees to warm up their flight muscles."},{"type":"why","q":"Why did Seeley study swarms on an island off Maine?","options":["It had no natural tree cavities for nests.","It had more bees than anywhere in America.","It was the only place queens would fly."],"answer":0},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author mention committees in the last paragraph?","options":["to show that bees once learned to vote from humans","to suggest people could decide things the way bees do","to argue that committees work better than swarms"],"answer":1}],"grade":6,"words":479,"auto":{"fk":7.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u2-c01","unit":"G6-U2","pool":"check","title":"Why Cities Run Hot","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"On a summer afternoon, a thermometer in the middle of a big city often reads several degrees higher than one in the countryside just a few miles away. This difference has a name: the urban heat island effect. It affects millions of people, and understanding it has become an important goal for city planners.\n\nThe main cause is the material cities are built from. Asphalt roads, dark rooftops and concrete sidewalks absorb sunlight during the day and store it as heat. A blacktop parking lot in full sun can become hot enough to burn bare feet, even when the air temperature is comfortable. After sunset, these surfaces slowly release the stored heat, which keeps the city warm long into the night. According to the Environmental Protection Agency, daytime temperatures in cities can be about one to seven degrees Fahrenheit higher than in nearby rural areas, and nighttime temperatures can be about two to five degrees higher.\n\nPlants play a large part in the difference. In the countryside, fields and forests cool the air through a process called evapotranspiration, in which plants release water vapor from their leaves. As the water evaporates, it carries heat away, much as sweat cools your skin. Trees also provide shade, which keeps the ground beneath them from heating up at all. In a city where most of the ground is paved, there are fewer plants to provide this natural cooling.\n\nHuman activity adds even more heat. Cars, buses, factories and air conditioners all release warmth into the air. Tall buildings close together can also trap heat and block the breezes that might otherwise carry it away.\n\nHeat islands are more than an inconvenience. During heat waves, high temperatures can be dangerous, especially for older adults, young children and people who work outdoors. Hot cities also use more electricity for air conditioning, which raises energy bills and can strain power systems.\n\nFortunately, cities are finding ways to cool down. One solution is the cool roof, a roof covered with a light-colored or reflective coating. Because it reflects much of the sunlight instead of absorbing it, a cool roof stays far cooler than a dark one on a sunny day. New York City has coated millions of square feet of rooftops with white paint through a program called NYC CoolRoofs. Other cities are planting trees along streets, building parks and adding green roofs, which are rooftops covered with soil and plants.\n\nSome solutions are as simple as rethinking color. Los Angeles has tested a gray coating on some streets that reflects more sunlight than ordinary blacktop. Early measurements showed that the coated streets were cooler on hot days than the uncoated ones.\n\nThe urban heat island effect is a problem that people created, but it is also one that people can solve. By choosing lighter materials and making room for plants, cities can become cooler, healthier places to live.","questions":[{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the claim that plants help keep an area cool?","options":["Cars, buses, factories and air conditioners all release warmth into the air.","Hot cities also use more electricity for air conditioning, which raises energy bills and can strain power systems.","As the water evaporates, it carries heat away, much as sweat cools your skin."],"answer":2,"evidence":"As the water evaporates, it carries heat away, much as sweat cools your skin."},{"type":"main","q":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Cities are hotter than rural areas for clear reasons, and people can reduce the heat.","Cities should stop building roads because asphalt is too costly to repair.","Rural areas are cooler because they receive less sunlight than cities do."],"answer":0},{"type":"literal","q":"What happens to the heat stored in city surfaces after sunset?","options":["It escapes all at once.","It is slowly released.","It turns into light."],"answer":1,"evidence":"After sunset, these surfaces slowly release the stored heat, which keeps the city warm long into the night."},{"type":"vocab","q":"In paragraph 3, what does \"evapotranspiration\" describe?","options":["sunlight heating up asphalt","wind moving between towers","plants releasing water vapor"],"answer":2,"evidence":"In the countryside, fields and forests cool the air through a process called evapotranspiration, in which plants release water vapor from their leaves."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author describe the program in New York City?","options":["to give a real example of a solution","to argue that roofs cause heat islands","to compare New York with Los Angeles"],"answer":0}],"grade":6,"words":483,"auto":{"fk":8.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u2-c02","unit":"G6-U2","pool":"check","title":"The Woman Who Measured the Universe","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"In the early 1900s, the Harvard College Observatory employed a group of women whose job title was \"computer.\" Long before electronic machines existed, these women did the careful, repetitive work of examining photographs of the night sky. They measured the brightness and position of thousands of stars. They recorded the results in notebooks, and they earned far less money than the men who ran the observatory. One of them, Henrietta Swan Leavitt, made a discovery that changed how humans understand the size of the universe.\n\nLeavitt joined the observatory in 1895 as a volunteer and later became a paid staff member. Her assignment was to study variable stars, which grow brighter and dimmer over time. She worked with glass photographic plates, each one showing a small patch of sky. By comparing plates taken on different nights, she could see which stars had changed. Over several years, she identified more than 2,400 variable stars, a remarkable number for the time.\n\nMany of the stars she studied were in the Small Magellanic Cloud. This hazy patch of light can be seen from the Southern Hemisphere. Because all of these stars sit in the same distant cloud, they are all roughly the same distance from Earth. Leavitt realized this gave her an unusual advantage. If one star in the cloud looked brighter than another, it really was brighter, not just closer.\n\nFocusing on a type of star now called a Cepheid variable, Leavitt noticed a pattern. The brighter a Cepheid was, the longer it took to complete one cycle from bright to dim and back again. In 1912, she published a short paper showing this relationship for twenty-five stars. The rule became known as the period-luminosity relationship, and it gave astronomers a powerful new tool.\n\nHere is why it mattered. Before Leavitt, astronomers had no reliable way to measure the distance to stars that were very far away. Her discovery meant that if they timed a Cepheid's cycle, they could figure out how bright it truly was. By comparing that true brightness with how bright it appeared from Earth, they could calculate how far away it was. The method works much like judging the distance of a streetlight at night if you already know how bright the bulb is.\n\nAbout a decade later, the astronomer Edwin Hubble used Leavitt's rule to measure the distance to Cepheids in the Andromeda Nebula. His results showed that Andromeda was far outside our own Milky Way. It was an entirely separate galaxy, and the universe was far larger than most scientists had believed.\n\nLeavitt did not live to see this result, and she received little recognition during her life. In the 1920s, a Swedish mathematician tried to nominate her for the Nobel Prize, not knowing she had passed away a few years earlier. Today, astronomers recognize her work as one of the foundations of modern astronomy. A crater on the moon and an asteroid now carry her name, honoring a scientist who measured the universe one photograph at a time.","questions":[{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that Leavitt was underappreciated in her own time?","options":["Over several years, she identified more than 2,400 variable stars, a remarkable number for the time.","Leavitt did not live to see this result, and she received little recognition during her life.","Her assignment was to study variable stars, which grow brighter and dimmer over time."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Leavitt did not live to see this result, and she received little recognition during her life."},{"type":"why","q":"Why was the Small Magellanic Cloud useful for Leavitt's research?","options":["Its stars are closer to Earth than any other stars.","Its stars never change in brightness over time.","Its stars are all about the same distance from Earth."],"answer":2},{"type":"literal","q":"What pattern did Leavitt notice in Cepheid variables?","options":["Brighter ones took longer to complete a cycle.","Brighter ones moved faster across the sky.","Brighter ones were always the closest stars."],"answer":0,"evidence":"The brighter a Cepheid was, the longer it took to complete one cycle from bright to dim and back again."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author compare the method to judging the distance of a streetlight?","options":["to show that astronomers studied city lights","to explain a hard idea with a familiar example","to argue that streetlights hide the stars"],"answer":1},{"type":"inference","q":"What does the passage suggest about Hubble's discovery?","options":["It proved that Leavitt's rule was wrong.","It was made before Leavitt's discovery.","It depended on Leavitt's earlier work."],"answer":2}],"grade":6,"words":505,"auto":{"fk":8.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u2-c03","unit":"G6-U2","pool":"check","title":"Let Teens Sleep","genre":"argument","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"Every school morning, millions of American teenagers drag themselves out of bed while it is still dark outside. Many of them will spend first period struggling to keep their eyes open. This is not because they are lazy or stayed up playing games. It is because their bodies are running on a different clock. Middle and high schools should start no earlier than 8:30 in the morning, and the evidence for this change is strong.\n\nThe first reason is biology. During the teenage years, the body begins releasing melatonin, the hormone that makes people sleepy, later at night than it did in childhood. As a result, many teens are not able to fall asleep until around 11 at night, no matter how early they go to bed. The American Academy of Sleep Medicine recommends that teens get eight to ten hours of sleep each night. A teenager who falls asleep at 11 and wakes at 6 for a 7:30 start gets only about seven hours, and many get even less.\n\nThe second reason is health and safety. Researchers have linked a lack of sleep in teens to problems with mood, attention and even physical health. Drowsy teens are also more likely to be involved in car accidents. In 2014, the American Academy of Pediatrics, a major organization of children's doctors, issued a policy statement recommending that middle and high schools start at 8:30 or later. The doctors described insufficient sleep in adolescents as an important public health issue.\n\nThe third reason is learning. When schools have moved their start times later, students have generally gotten more sleep. A study in Seattle, which pushed its high school start time from 7:50 to 8:45 in 2016, found that students slept about thirty-four more minutes per night. Their grades in the class studied also improved, and attendance at first period got better.\n\nSome people argue that later start times would cause problems. Buses might have to run on new schedules, and after-school sports and jobs could end later in the evening. Parents who need to be at work early might find it harder to drop their children off. These concerns are real, but they are problems of planning, not reasons to ignore students' health. Many districts have solved them by changing bus routes or adjusting practice schedules.\n\nCalifornia has already acted on the evidence. In 2019, it became the first state to pass a law requiring later start times, and the rule took effect in 2022. Most public high schools there may no longer begin before 8:30, and most middle schools may not begin before 8:00.\n\nSchools exist to help students learn and grow. Asking teenagers to learn at an hour when their brains are still half asleep works against that goal. A later bell is a simple, research-based change, and it is one that more communities should make.","questions":[{"type":"claim","q":"What is the author's main claim?","options":["Middle and high schools should start at 8:30 or later.","Teenagers should go to bed earlier on school nights.","Schools should cancel their after-school sports."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the claim that later start times help students learn?","options":["Buses might have to run on new schedules, and after-school sports and jobs could end later in the evening.","Their grades in the class studied also improved, and attendance at first period got better.","Drowsy teens are also more likely to be involved in car accidents."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Their grades in the class studied also improved, and attendance at first period got better."},{"type":"claim","q":"How does the author respond to the concerns about buses and sports?","options":["by agreeing that schools should keep early times","by saying that buses and sports are unimportant","by calling them planning problems that can be solved"],"answer":2},{"type":"vocab","q":"What does \"insufficient\" most likely mean in paragraph 3?","options":["too little","too deep","too noisy"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The doctors described insufficient sleep in adolescents as an important public health issue."},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author include the details about California?","options":["to prove that California students sleep most","to show that the change is already happening","to explain why buses run late in the West"],"answer":1}],"grade":6,"words":479,"auto":{"fk":7.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u3-p01","unit":"G6-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Saving Coral Reefs","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: Why Reefs Turn White\n\nCoral reefs look like colorful rocks, but they are built by living animals. Each coral is made of tiny creatures called polyps, which build hard skeletons out of limestone. Inside the polyps live microscopic algae. The algae use sunlight to make food, and they share much of it with the coral. In return, the coral gives the algae a protected home. The algae also give many corals their bright colors.\n\nThis partnership is delicate. When ocean water stays too warm for too long, the stressed corals expel their algae. Without the algae, the coral's white skeleton shows through its clear tissue. This is called coral bleaching. A bleached coral is not yet lost, and it can recover if the water cools quickly enough. If the heat lasts for weeks, however, the coral may starve.\n\nBleaching matters because reefs support an enormous amount of life. Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they are home to about a quarter of all known marine species. Reefs also protect coastlines by breaking up powerful waves before they reach the shore. As ocean temperatures have risen, large bleaching events have become more frequent around the world, including several on Australia's Great Barrier Reef.\n\n## Text 2: A Day in the Coral Nursery\n\nAt eight in the morning, I pulled on my fins and dropped into the clear water off the Florida Keys. Twenty feet below me stood what looks like a forest of plastic Christmas trees. Hanging from their branches were hundreds of small pieces of staghorn coral, each one tied on with fishing line. This is a coral nursery, and I am one of the volunteers who help take care of it.\n\nOur job today was cleaning. Algae and snails grow on the nursery trees, and if we leave them, they can crowd out the young corals. I scrubbed each branch gently with a small brush, careful not to touch the corals themselves. Then my diving partner measured a few pieces and wrote the numbers on an underwater slate.\n\nThe idea behind the nursery is simple. When a branch of staghorn coral breaks off, it can keep growing on its own. Scientists cut small fragments from healthy corals and grow them on the trees, where they are safe from the sand and waves. After several months, the fragments are large enough to be planted back onto damaged reefs.\n\nLater in the week, we will take a batch of grown corals to a nearby reef. We will attach them to the rock with a special epoxy, a kind of underwater glue, and hope they settle in. Some will not survive another hot summer. But every patch that takes hold gives fish a place to hide and helps the reef rebuild itself.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"How does Text 2 build on the information in Text 1?","options":["It shows one way people help reefs recover from damage.","It argues that bleaching is no longer a serious problem.","It explains why algae give corals their bright colors."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence from Text 1 best explains why reefs matter so much to ocean life?","options":["As ocean temperatures have risen, large bleaching events have become more frequent around the world, including several on Australia's Great Barrier Reef.","Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they are home to about a quarter of all known marine species.","Each coral is made of tiny creatures called polyps, which build hard skeletons out of limestone."],"answer":1,"evidence":"Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they are home to about a quarter of all known marine species."},{"type":"literal","q":"According to Text 2, why are coral fragments grown on nursery trees?","options":["They get more sunlight near the top.","They are hidden from hungry divers.","They are safe from the sand and waves."],"answer":2,"evidence":"Scientists cut small fragments from healthy corals and grow them on the trees, where they are safe from the sand and waves."},{"type":"inference","q":"Based on both texts, why might some replanted corals fail to survive?","options":["Warm water could cause them to bleach again.","Divers might scrub them much too hard.","The epoxy usually washes away quickly."],"answer":0}],"grade":6,"words":467,"auto":{"fk":6.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u3-p02","unit":"G6-U3","pool":"practice","title":"Wolves Return to Yellowstone","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: A Missing Piece\n\nGray wolves once roamed across much of North America, including the land that became Yellowstone National Park in 1872. As ranches spread across the West, people saw wolves as a threat to cattle and sheep. Hunters and government trappers removed wolves from the park, and by the late 1920s, Yellowstone's wolves were gone.\n\nWithout wolves, the park's elk had few predators besides bears and cougars. Elk herds grew large, and in winter they gathered along streams, where they nibbled young willow and aspen trees down to stubs. Some scientists argued that the park's ecosystem, the community of living things and their surroundings, was out of balance.\n\nIn 1995, after years of debate, biologists brought fourteen wolves from Canada to Yellowstone. They kept the wolves in large pens for several weeks so the animals could adjust, then opened the gates. Seventeen more wolves arrived in 1996. The wolves formed packs, raised pups and spread across the park.\n\nOver the next twenty years, researchers noticed changes. Elk numbers in the northern part of the park dropped, and elk spent less time standing in open valleys near streams. In some places, willows grew taller, and beavers returned to build dams. Many people describe this as a chain reaction that started with wolves. However, scientists still debate how much credit wolves deserve. Bears, cougars, human hunters and dry years also affected the elk, and willows recovered in some streams but not in others.\n\n## Text 2: Dawn in the Lamar Valley\n\nMy grandmother woke me at five o'clock, when the sky over the Lamar Valley was still gray. By the time we parked at a pullout along the road, a dozen people were already there, standing behind spotting scopes on tripods. Nobody spoke above a whisper.\n\n\"Wolf watchers,\" Grandma said. \"Some of them come every morning.\"\n\nA woman in a wool hat let me look through her scope. At first, I saw only frosty grass and a line of bare willows along the river. Then a dark shape moved across the hillside, then another, and then four more. The wolves trotted in a loose line, noses down, as if they had somewhere important to be.\n\nThe woman told me she had been watching this valley since the late 1990s. \"When I first came,\" she said, \"the elk stood around in the river bottoms all day, and the willows were barely knee-high. Now look at them.\" In some spots, the willows along the river were taller than a person, though in other spots they were still short.\n\nShe said some of the wolves wear GPS collars so park biologists can follow where each pack travels. A lot of what researchers know also comes from careful watchers like her, who write down what they see each day and share their notes with scientists.\n\nWe watched until the wolves disappeared over a ridge. On the drive back, Grandma said she had visited the park as a girl, when there was not a single wolf in it. \"It looked the same,\" she said, \"but it was missing something. Now it feels whole again.\"","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"Which idea appears in both texts?","options":["Wolves were brought to the park from Canada.","Willows by the streams grew taller in some places.","Wolf watchers share their notes with scientists."],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence from Text 1 best supports the idea that other causes also shaped the changes in the park?","options":["Bears, cougars, human hunters and dry years also affected the elk, and willows recovered in some streams but not in others.","They kept the wolves in large pens for several weeks so the animals could adjust, then opened the gates.","Elk herds grew large, and in winter they gathered along streams, where they nibbled young willow and aspen trees down to stubs."],"answer":0,"evidence":"Bears, cougars, human hunters and dry years also affected the elk, and willows recovered in some streams but not in others."},{"type":"inference","q":"Based on both texts, why were the willows barely knee-high when the woman first came?","options":["Spring floods had washed them away.","Wolf watchers had trampled them.","Large herds of elk kept eating them."],"answer":2},{"type":"compare","q":"How does Text 2 add to the information in Text 1?","options":["It explains why ranchers wanted wolves gone from the West.","It gives a firsthand view of the wolves and the park today.","It lists the exact number of wolves in the park this year."],"answer":1}],"grade":6,"words":520,"auto":{"fk":5.9,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u3-c01","unit":"G6-U3","pool":"check","title":"Who Invented Printing?","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: Gutenberg's Press\n\nAround the year 1450, in the German city of Mainz, a goldsmith named Johannes Gutenberg developed a new way to make books. Before his invention, most books in Europe were copied by hand, a process that could take months for a single volume. Books were so rare and expensive that only churches, universities and wealthy families owned many of them.\n\nGutenberg's system combined several ideas. He cast individual letters in metal using a hand mold, so that each letter could be made quickly and in large numbers. These pieces, called movable type, could be arranged into words and lines, inked and pressed onto paper. After a page was printed, the type could be taken apart and reused. He also developed an oil-based ink that stuck well to metal, and he adapted a screw press, similar to the kind used for squeezing grapes, to press the paper evenly.\n\nHis most famous work, the Gutenberg Bible, was completed in the mid-1450s. About 180 copies were printed, and fewer than fifty survive today. Within fifty years, printing shops had spread to more than 200 European cities, and millions of books had been produced. Ideas could now travel faster and farther than ever before, which helped fuel the growth of science and education across Europe.\n\n## Text 2: Printing Before Gutenberg\n\nMany people believe printing began with Gutenberg, but its history in Asia is centuries older. In China, printers were carving entire pages into wooden blocks by the 700s. The oldest dated printed book that survives, a Buddhist text called the Diamond Sutra, was printed in China in the year 868.\n\nMovable type was also invented first in China. Around the 1040s, a commoner named Bi Sheng created individual characters out of baked clay. A scholar named Shen Kuo described his method in detail, explaining how the characters were arranged in an iron frame and could be reused. However, the Chinese writing system uses thousands of different characters, which made movable type less practical than in languages with small alphabets. Woodblock printing remained more common in China for centuries.\n\nIn Korea, printers took the idea further by casting type in metal. In 1377, Korean monks printed a Buddhist book known as the Jikji using metal movable type. That was more than seventy years before the Gutenberg Bible. A copy of the Jikji is now kept at the National Library of France, and UNESCO recognizes it as the oldest surviving book printed with metal movable type.\n\nHistorians still debate how much contact, if any, existed between Asian and European printers. What is clear is that the idea of printing was not born in a single place or in a single moment.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"How does Text 2 change the picture given in Text 1?","options":["It shows that Gutenberg copied his press from Korean monks.","It shows that European books cost less than Asian ones.","It shows that printing developed in Asia long before Gutenberg."],"answer":2},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that metal type was used before Gutenberg?","options":["In 1377, Korean monks printed a Buddhist book known as the Jikji using metal movable type.","He also developed an oil-based ink that stuck well to metal, and he adapted a screw press, similar to the kind used for squeezing grapes, to press the paper evenly.","A scholar named Shen Kuo described his method in detail, explaining how the characters were arranged in an iron frame and could be reused."],"answer":0,"evidence":"In 1377, Korean monks printed a Buddhist book known as the Jikji using metal movable type."},{"type":"compare","q":"Based on both texts, why did movable type catch on faster in Europe than in China?","options":["European printers had better paper.","European alphabets use far fewer symbols.","Chinese rulers banned printed books."],"answer":1},{"type":"literal","q":"What material did Bi Sheng use to make his type?","options":["carved wood","cast iron","baked clay"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Around the 1040s, a commoner named Bi Sheng created individual characters out of baked clay."},{"type":"main","q":"Which statement best combines the main ideas of both texts?","options":["Printing grew in several places, and Gutenberg's press spread it in Europe.","Gutenberg invented every form of printing, and Asia later used his ideas.","Printing began in Korea, and Europe ignored it for a thousand years."],"answer":0}],"grade":6,"words":451,"auto":{"fk":8.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u3-c02","unit":"G6-U3","pool":"check","title":"Volcanoes and Energy","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: How Volcanoes Form\n\nDeep beneath Earth's surface, temperatures are high enough to melt rock. This melted rock, called magma, is lighter than the solid rock around it, so it slowly rises toward the surface. When magma escapes through an opening in the crust, it is called lava, and the opening is a volcano.\n\nMost volcanoes form near the edges of tectonic plates, the giant slabs of rock that make up Earth's outer shell. At some boundaries, plates pull apart, and magma rises to fill the gap. At others, one plate slides beneath another and melts as it sinks into the hot interior. A few volcanoes, such as those in Hawaii, form above hot spots, where plumes of heat rise from deep within the planet. Scientists estimate that about 1,350 potentially active volcanoes exist around the world. Many of them lie along the Ring of Fire, a horseshoe-shaped zone around the Pacific Ocean.\n\nVolcanoes can be destructive, but they also create. Lava that cools and hardens forms new land, and volcanic ash breaks down into some of the richest soil on Earth. The heat that drives volcanoes also warms underground water, producing hot springs and geysers. Scientists call this underground heat geothermal energy, from Greek words meaning \"earth\" and \"heat.\"\n\n## Text 2: The Island That Runs on Heat\n\nIceland sits on top of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. This is a boundary where the North American and Eurasian plates are slowly pulling apart. The country has more than thirty active volcanic systems, and eruptions happen every few years. For Icelanders, though, the heat underground is also one of the country's greatest resources.\n\nAbout nine out of ten homes in Iceland are heated with geothermal energy. Wells drilled into the ground bring up hot water and steam, which flow through pipes to houses, schools and swimming pools. In the capital, Reykjavik, some sidewalks and streets have hot water pipes beneath them to melt snow in winter. Geothermal power plants also use steam to spin turbines and generate electricity. Together with hydropower from rivers and waterfalls, geothermal energy supplies nearly all of Iceland's electricity. Underground heat even helps Icelanders grow food. Greenhouses warmed by hot water from the ground allow farmers to raise tomatoes, cucumbers and even bananas, despite long, dark winters near the Arctic Circle.\n\nThis was not always the case. In the early twentieth century, Iceland relied heavily on imported coal and oil for heat. Over the following decades, the country invested in drilling and pipelines, and today the air over Reykjavik is far cleaner than it once was.\n\nLiving near volcanoes still comes with risks. In 2010, the eruption of a volcano called Eyjafjallajokull sent a cloud of ash into the sky that stopped air travel across much of Europe for days. Icelanders monitor their volcanoes closely, using sensors that detect small earthquakes and changes in the ground.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"Which idea connects the two texts?","options":["Volcanoes form only in places that are very cold.","The heat behind volcanoes can be a useful resource.","Most volcanoes are found in the middle of plates."],"answer":1},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence from Text 2 best supports the idea that geothermal energy is widely used in Iceland?","options":["The country has more than thirty active volcanic systems, and eruptions happen every few years.","Icelanders monitor their volcanoes closely, using sensors that detect small earthquakes and changes in the ground.","About nine out of ten homes in Iceland are heated with geothermal energy."],"answer":2,"evidence":"About nine out of ten homes in Iceland are heated with geothermal energy."},{"type":"inference","q":"Using Text 1, why does Iceland have so much underground heat?","options":["It sits on a boundary where plates pull apart.","It sits above a plate that is sinking quickly.","It sits far from any tectonic plate boundary."],"answer":0},{"type":"vocab","q":"In Text 1, what does \"geothermal\" mean?","options":["having to do with the motion of oceans","having to do with heat inside the earth","having to do with sunlight on the land"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Scientists call this underground heat geothermal energy, from Greek words meaning \"earth\" and \"heat.\""},{"type":"author","q":"Why does the author of Text 2 mention the 2010 eruption?","options":["to explain how Iceland first found geothermal heat","to prove that ash makes Iceland's soil very rich","to show that living near volcanoes still has risks"],"answer":2}],"grade":6,"words":480,"auto":{"fk":8.6,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-u3-c03","unit":"G6-U3","pool":"check","title":"Libraries Then and Now","genre":"informational","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","text":"## Text 1: A Brief History of the Public Library\n\nFor most of history, books were too expensive for ordinary people to own. In 1731, Benjamin Franklin and a group of friends in Philadelphia found a solution. They pooled their money to buy books that members could borrow, creating the Library Company of Philadelphia. It was one of the first lending libraries in the American colonies, and it still exists today.\n\nThe idea of a library that was free to everyone took longer to develop. In 1848, Boston established one of the first large public libraries in the United States paid for by taxes. The Boston Public Library opened its doors in 1854, with the belief that free access to books was essential for citizens in a democracy.\n\nThe greatest expansion came from the businessman Andrew Carnegie. Carnegie's family was poor when they moved from Scotland to Pennsylvania. As a working boy, he educated himself by borrowing books from a local man who opened his private collection to young workers. After he became one of the richest people in the world, Carnegie paid to build more than 2,500 libraries, more than 1,600 of them in the United States. Towns had to promise to provide land and pay for the library's upkeep. Many of these buildings are still in use, and they helped make the public library a part of nearly every American community.\n\n## Text 2: What You Can Borrow Now\n\nLast week, a patron asked me whether our library had a sewing machine. I walked her to the back room and handed her one, along with a card explaining how long she could keep it. She looked at me as if I had performed a magic trick.\n\nPeople are often surprised by what libraries lend today. At our branch, you can check out a telescope, a ukulele, a set of cake pans or a device that provides internet access at home. Some libraries lend seeds for gardening, tools for home repairs or even passes to museums and state parks. The collections may look strange, but the reason behind them is simple. Not everyone can afford to buy something they will only use a few times, and sharing makes more sense than everyone buying their own.\n\nOf course, books are still at the heart of what we do. But we have also become a place where people apply for jobs, practice English, learn to code and get help filling out forms. On weekday afternoons, our study rooms fill with students who need a quiet place and a reliable internet connection.\n\nEvery so often, someone tells me libraries are outdated because everything is online now. I usually invite that person to visit on a Saturday morning, when every computer is busy and every chair is taken.","questions":[{"type":"compare","q":"Which idea do both texts share?","options":["Libraries give people access to things they can't afford.","Libraries should stop lending books and lend tools.","Libraries were built mainly to serve rich families."],"answer":0},{"type":"evidence","q":"Which sentence best supports the idea that libraries are still heavily used today?","options":["They pooled their money to buy books that members could borrow, creating the Library Company of Philadelphia.","I usually invite that person to visit on a Saturday morning, when every computer is busy and every chair is taken.","At our branch, you can check out a telescope, a ukulele, a set of cake pans or a device that provides internet access at home."],"answer":1,"evidence":"I usually invite that person to visit on a Saturday morning, when every computer is busy and every chair is taken."},{"type":"compare","q":"How does Text 2 connect to Carnegie's childhood in Text 1?","options":["Both show libraries charging fees for new buildings.","Both show libraries lending instruments to children.","Both show libraries helping people who lack money."],"answer":2},{"type":"literal","q":"When did the Boston Public Library first open its doors?","options":["in 1854","in 1731","in 1848"],"answer":0,"evidence":"The Boston Public Library opened its doors in 1854, with the belief that free access to books was essential for citizens in a democracy."},{"type":"author","q":"What is the main purpose of Text 2?","options":["to explain how Andrew Carnegie paid for libraries","to show how libraries have grown to meet new needs","to persuade readers to buy their own sewing machines"],"answer":1}],"grade":6,"words":463,"auto":{"fk":8.7,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-book-ch1","unit":"G6-U4","pool":"book","title":"The Plate by the Door","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","book":"The Birchwood Time Capsule","chapter":1,"text":"Amira Saleh had lived in Birchwood, Minnesota, for exactly nineteen days, and she had counted every one of them. In Detroit, she had known every crack in the sidewalk between her apartment and her school. In Birchwood, the sidewalks gave up after three blocks, the lake froze so hard that people drove trucks across it, and every kid in the sixth grade had known every other kid since kindergarten.\n\nNobody was unkind to her; they simply already had their friendships, their private jokes and their usual tables in the lunchroom. Amira had learned to eat quickly and spend recess on the front steps of the school, where it was quiet.\n\nThe school was a tall brick building on Elm Street with the year 1924 carved above the door. It was also running out of time. In June, every student would move into a modern building near the highway, and a demolition crew would knock the old school down to make room for apartments. Most kids were excited about the new gymnasium, but Amira didn't care either way, since one more move hardly seemed to matter.\n\nOn a gray Monday in April, she was sitting on the steps when a chunk of old snow slid off the railing and landed beside her. It knocked loose a clump of brown leaves that had been stuck against the wall for who knew how long. Behind the leaves, set low in the bricks, was a small metal plate she had never noticed. It was green with age, and the letters were nearly worn away.\n\nAmira knelt and rubbed the plate with her mitten until she could slowly read the faded words aloud.\n\n\"The Class of 1926 has hidden a gift for the children of 2026. Look where the tall one stands, and walk toward the bend.\"\n\nShe read it twice more, then counted the years on her fingers, even though she didn't really need to. It was 2026. Whatever the gift was, it was meant for kids like her, and in nine weeks the building would be gone.\n\nAfter recess, she waited until the classroom emptied and told her teacher, Mr. Sandoval. He followed her outside, crouched beside the plate and gave a low whistle.\n\nHe explained that during his first year at the school, an elderly custodian had told him stories about a buried box, but nobody had ever discovered anything. Most people decided it was just a legend.\n\nWhen Amira asked where the plate could have come from, then, Mr. Sandoval had no answer.\n\nA voice behind them said, \"Is that a treasure map?\" Amira turned to see Felix Larsen, a boy from her class with a red stocking cap and a reputation for never running out of words. He had come back for his forgotten backpack, and now he was staring at the plate as if it were made of gold.\n\nAmira explained that it was a clue, not a map, and Felix declared that clues were even better. He studied the words with his lips moving. \"The tall one has to be a tree,\" he said, \"and the bend must be Birch Creek, which curves right behind the ball field.\"\n\nAmira looked across the schoolyard, which held a few young maples beside the fence, a swing set and a muddy ball field, but no tall tree anywhere.\n\nFelix didn't seem worried. His great-grandmother's neighbor, Ingrid Lindqvist, was ninety-six years old and remembered everything, he said, and her older brother had attended this very school a hundred years ago. He grinned at Amira. \"Want to go ask her tomorrow?\"\n\nAmira opened her mouth to say no, the way she had said no to everything in Birchwood so far. Instead, she heard herself say, \"Okay.\"","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"Where did Amira find the metal plate?","options":["under the old swing set","on the gym wall inside","set low in the bricks"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Behind the leaves, set low in the bricks, was a small metal plate she had never noticed."},{"type":"vocab","q":"Based on the chapter, what is a \"legend\"?","options":["a map of the oldest streets","a story that may be untrue","a kind of metal wall sign"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Most people decided it was just a legend."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why does the clue on the plate matter so much to Amira right away?","options":["The gift is meant for her year, and the school will soon be gone.","The plate was made by her own family when they lived in Birchwood.","Her teacher promised a prize to the first student who solves it."],"answer":0},{"type":"craft","q":"How does the author show a change in Amira at the end of the chapter?","options":["by having her laugh at one of Felix's jokes","by having her walk home alone again","by having her say yes instead of no"],"answer":2}],"grade":6,"words":625,"auto":{"fk":6.3,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-book-ch2","unit":"G6-U4","pool":"book","title":"Arne's Notebook","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","book":"The Birchwood Time Capsule","chapter":2,"text":"Ingrid Lindqvist lived in a yellow house at the edge of the lake, with a porch full of flowerpots that were still empty from winter. She answered the door with a cane in one hand and a plate of cardamom cookies in the other, as if she had been expecting company for years.\n\nShe greeted Felix by his full name, guessed correctly that Amira must be the new girl from Detroit and hurried them both inside before the warm air could escape.\n\nHer living room was warm and crowded with photographs. Amira sat on the edge of a flowered couch while Felix explained about the metal plate, talking so fast that he had to stop twice to breathe. Ingrid listened without interrupting. When he finished, she set down her teacup very carefully.\n\n\"So it's real after all,\" she said softly, gazing toward the window. \"I always wondered whether Arne was exaggerating.\"\n\nShe told them that her brother Arne had been ten years old in 1926. Ingrid was born years after the box was buried, but Arne talked about it all through her childhood. His teacher, Miss Aune, had asked each student to write a letter to the children who would sit in their seats a hundred years later. Then the class sealed the letters in a copper box and buried it somewhere on the school grounds.\n\n\"Arne was the smallest boy in his class,\" Ingrid said, smiling. \"Miss Aune gave him the job of pacing off the steps to the hiding place, because he wanted so badly to be important. He was terribly proud of it.\"\n\nFelix asked whether she knew where the class had buried the box.\n\nIngrid shook her head. Arne had made a point of never telling anyone, she explained, because the box was supposed to be a surprise for the future. \"He passed away many years ago, and I assumed the secret had disappeared along with him,\" she said. Then she paused, as if remembering something important, and asked them to wait.\n\nShe went down the hallway and came back with a shoebox tied with string. Inside were old report cards, a harmonica and a thin school notebook with a brown cover. On the cover, in careful pencil, someone had written Arne Lindqvist, Grade 5, 1926.\n\nAmira turned the brittle pages slowly. There were spelling lists, arithmetic problems and a drawing of a horse. Near the back, she found a page that made her catch her breath. It was a sketch of the schoolyard. She recognized the shape of the building and the curve of the creek behind the ball field. To the left of the front steps stood an enormous tree, drawn with dozens of tiny pencil needles. Beneath the drawing, Arne had written a short rhyme.\n\nFrom the great pine's roots, face the bend.\nForty steps, and the search will end.\n\n\"The tall one is a pine tree,\" Felix whispered, practically bouncing on the couch, \"exactly like I predicted!\"\n\nAmira studied the drawing again before looking up at Ingrid, because something about it bothered her. \"There's no pine tree beside the steps anymore,\" she said. \"There's only the gravel parking lot.\"\n\nIngrid nodded slowly and explained that the town had cut the pine down when she was a girl, after lightning split its trunk during a summer storm and people worried it would topple onto the school. She looked at the two of them over her glasses. \"So your starting point has vanished,\" she said. \"That's a genuine problem.\"\n\nFelix slumped against the couch cushions, but Amira kept staring at the sketch. For the first time since moving to Birchwood, she felt something besides loneliness: she felt curious.\n\nShe asked politely whether they could borrow the notebook and promised to be extremely careful with it.\n\nIngrid placed the notebook in Amira's hands and closed her fingers around it. \"Arne would have liked you,\" she said. \"He always said the best secrets are the ones worth working for.\"","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What job did Miss Aune give Arne?","options":["pacing off the steps","sealing the copper box","writing the class rhyme"],"answer":0,"evidence":"Miss Aune gave him the job of pacing off the steps to the hiding place, because he wanted so badly to be important."},{"type":"vocab","q":"Based on the chapter, what does \"brittle\" mean?","options":["shiny and bright as new","thick and hard to lift","dry and easy to break"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Amira turned the brittle pages slowly."},{"type":"inference","q":"Why is the missing pine tree a problem for Amira and Felix?","options":["The tree is where Ingrid hid the notebook.","The rhyme tells them to start at the tree.","The tree held the key that opens the box."],"answer":1},{"type":"inference","q":"What does Ingrid mean when she says the best secrets are the ones worth working for?","options":["Solving a hard puzzle makes the reward feel greater.","Secrets should always be shared with others right away.","Old notebooks are worth more money than new ones."],"answer":0}],"grade":6,"words":663,"auto":{"fk":5.9,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-book-ch3","unit":"G6-U4","pool":"book","title":"Where the Pine Stood","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","book":"The Birchwood Time Capsule","chapter":3,"text":"Ingrid had given them one more thing before they left her house: a photograph from her album. It showed Arne's whole class lined up on the school steps in 1926, twenty-three children squinting into the sun. Behind them, to the left of the door, rose a pine tree so tall that its top was cut off by the edge of the picture.\n\nThe next afternoon, Amira and Felix stood in the school parking lot with the photograph, Arne's notebook and a box of sidewalk chalk.\n\nAmira had worked out a plan during math class. The building had not moved in a hundred years, she reasoned, so if they stood exactly where the photographer had stood, the vanished tree should line up with the same windows.\n\nIn the picture, the trunk of the pine stood directly in front of the third window from the left. Amira held the photograph at arm's length and walked slowly backward across the lot, comparing the old picture to the real building. When the windows and the door matched, she stopped. Then she lined up the trunk in the photo with the real wall and pointed. Felix walked where she pointed until she shouted, \"Stop!\"\n\nHe looked down in surprise. Under his sneakers, the pavement was cracked in a rough circle, and the cracks bulged upward in low, uneven ridges.\n\nFelix announced that this had to be the spot, then drew an enormous chalk X and performed a celebration dance around it.\n\nNext, they faced the bend in Birch Creek and counted forty long steps. The steps carried them across the parking lot, through a gap in the fence and onto the corner of the ball field, near the rusty backstop. Felix scratched a second X in the dirt with the heel of his shoe.\n\n\"We actually found it,\" he said, his voice full of wonder.\n\nAmira reminded him that they had only found a place to dig, which was not exactly the same thing, but she was smiling, too.\n\nTheir excitement lasted until they reached the principal's office. Ms. Brandt listened politely, looked at the notebook and sighed.\n\n\"This is wonderful detective work,\" she said, \"but I can't allow two students to dig holes on school property. The town owns this land, and the demolition crew arrives the day after school lets out in June. If you want permission, you'll have to ask the town council, which meets on Thursday night.\"\n\nFelix's face fell. On Thursday he would be in Duluth for his sister's hockey tournament, and his parents had already paid for the hotel.\n\nThey both turned to Amira at the same moment.\n\nHer stomach dropped. In three weeks at her new school, she had not raised her hand a single time, and the thought of standing in front of a room full of grown-ups made her fingers go cold.\n\nThat night, she sat at the kitchen table with the notebook while her mother stirred a pot of lentil soup. Amira explained everything, then admitted quietly that she couldn't possibly do it, since Felix was the one who enjoyed talking.\n\nHer mother turned down the stove and sat beside her. \"Felix knew about the creek,\" she said. \"But who found the plate? Who figured out the windows?\" She tapped the notebook. \"Those children wrote letters to people they would never meet. They were brave enough to hope somebody would listen. Maybe somebody should be brave enough to answer.\"\n\nAmira didn't sleep much that night. In the morning, she stood in front of the bathroom mirror and practiced what she would say. Her voice shook the first time, and it shook a little less the fifth time. By the tenth time, she had made up her mind: she would go to the meeting.","questions":[{"type":"sequence","q":"What did Amira and Felix do right after Felix drew the chalk X?","options":["showed the old photograph to the principal","faced the creek bend and counted forty steps","dug a small hole beside the rusty backstop"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Next, they faced the bend in Birch Creek and counted forty long steps."},{"type":"inference","q":"What most likely caused the ring of cracks and ridges in the pavement?","options":["the roots of the old pine","heavy trucks parked for years","ice freezing under the lot"],"answer":0},{"type":"why","q":"Why must Amira be the one to speak at the town council meeting?","options":["The principal chose Amira to speak for the class.","Ingrid is too tired to come to the meeting.","Felix will be away at a hockey tournament."],"answer":2},{"type":"theme","q":"What does Amira's mother mean when she says somebody should be brave enough to answer?","options":["Amira should write a long letter back to Ingrid Lindqvist.","Amira should speak up for the children who wrote the letters.","Amira should call Felix and ask him to stay in Birchwood."],"answer":1}],"grade":6,"words":630,"auto":{"fk":5.4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}},{"id":"g6-book-ch4","unit":"G6-U4","pool":"book","title":"For the Children of 2026","genre":"narrative","source":"app-original","license":"app-original","book":"The Birchwood Time Capsule","chapter":4,"text":"The town hall smelled like coffee and wet boots. Rows of folding chairs faced a long table where five council members sat behind name cards and stacks of paperwork, and Amira took a seat in the back row, trying to make herself as small as possible.\n\nFor forty minutes, the council talked about snowplows, a new stop sign and a broken water pipe. Then the mayor, a tall woman named Gloria Pike, looked at her list. \"Next is a request from a student. Amira Saleh?\"\n\nAmira walked to the microphone on legs that felt like water. She unfolded her notes, and for one terrible moment, every word on the page looked like a stranger.\n\nThen she noticed someone familiar in the second row. Ingrid Lindqvist was sitting very straight, with both hands folded on top of her cane, and when she caught Amira's eye, she nodded once, firmly.\n\nAmira took a deep breath and began. She described the metal plate, Miss Aune's class and Arne's notebook, then held up the photograph and explained how she had matched the windows to locate the vanished tree. Her voice wobbled at first, but the longer she talked, the steadier it grew. \"Those kids wrote to us a hundred years ago,\" she finished. \"This is the year it's supposed to be opened, and it's the last spring the school will be standing. I think we owe them an answer.\"\n\nThe room was silent. Then Ingrid began to clap, slowly, and others joined in. The council voted five to zero to allow a supervised dig on Saturday morning.\n\nBy Saturday, the news had spread all over Birchwood. Half the town stood around the corner of the ball field in the cold sunshine. Felix had come home from Duluth a day early, and he kept saying he couldn't believe he had missed the meeting. Two workers from the town brought shovels and a small digger.\n\nThey started digging at the X near the backstop. Two feet down, the shovels scraped against gravel, and three feet down, they reached sticky clay. At four feet, there was still nothing but more clay, and the disappointed crowd began to murmur as a few people drifted back toward their cars.\n\nAmira stared at the empty hole and then at the notebook until she suddenly remembered something Ingrid had mentioned. \"Arne was the smallest boy in his class,\" she said out loud. \"He was only ten. His steps were much shorter than ours!\"\n\nMr. Sandoval understood at once. He waved the kids from Amira's class forward, and she led them back to the cracked circle of pavement. Together, they walked toward the bend in small, careful steps, counting out loud. Forty short steps ended several yards before the first hole, just inside the old fence.\n\nThe workers started digging again, and less than three feet down, a shovel struck something with a dull, hollow clang.\n\nIt was a copper box, turned green with age. When the mayor lifted its lid, the crowd pressed closer. Inside, wrapped in oilcloth, were twenty-three letters, a few drawings, a glass marble and a photograph of the great pine. Amira found one envelope in particular and placed it in Ingrid's hands. On the front, in careful pencil, it said: From Arne Lindqvist, who counted the steps.\n\nIngrid read it aloud, her voice thin but clear. Arne wrote that he hoped Birchwood was still a friendly town, where people looked out for each other and welcomed anyone new.\n\nAmira looked around while Felix stood beside her, grinning. Kids whose names she had barely learned were crowding in to read the letters over her shoulder, asking her questions as if she had always belonged there. Ingrid reached up and squeezed her hand.\n\nThat spring, before the old school came down, Amira's class wrote letters of their own. They sealed them in a new box for the children of 2126, to be buried at the new school. When it was time to write the clue, the whole class agreed that Amira should be the one to write it.","questions":[{"type":"literal","q":"What did the workers find under the second spot they dug?","options":["a glass jar full of drawings","a metal plate with a new clue","a copper box green with age"],"answer":2,"evidence":"It was a copper box, turned green with age."},{"type":"why","q":"Why was there nothing but clay in the first hole?","options":["The box had been moved by the old custodian.","It was measured with longer steps than Arne's.","The workers dug on the wrong side of the creek."],"answer":1},{"type":"theme","q":"Which statement best expresses a theme of the novel?","options":["Finding the courage to speak up can help you belong.","Old buildings should never be torn down for new ones.","Secrets are best when they stay hidden forever."],"answer":0},{"type":"inference","q":"How does Arne's letter connect to Amira's experience in Birchwood?","options":["He wrote about moving to Birchwood from Detroit too.","He described feeling lonely as the smallest boy.","He hoped for a town that welcomes newcomers like her."],"answer":2}],"grade":6,"words":680,"auto":{"fk":5.4,"decodable":null,"checks":"pass"}}];
